\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces A transverse light clock with mirror separation $\ell $ and $\beta =3/5$. These are spatial projections, with the time of each event written beside it: emission $O$, reflection $M$, and return $R$. Choose the first spatial axis along ${\bm  {\mathrm  {u}}}$ and the second along the mirrors. In the clock's rest frame the pulse retraces its path; in the unprimed frame each dotted leg has length $5\ell /4$. Light speed is $1$ in both frames, so the same round trip takes $\Delta t=5\ell /2$ while the clock records $\Delta s=2\ell $.}}{32}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:observer-light-clock}{{2}{32}{A transverse light clock with mirror separation $\ell $ and $\beta =3/5$. These are spatial projections, with the time of each event written beside it: emission $O$, reflection $M$, and return $R$. Choose the first spatial axis along $\bu $ and the second along the mirrors. In the clock's rest frame the pulse retraces its path; in the unprimed frame each dotted leg has length $5\ell /4$. Light speed is $1$ in both frames, so the same round trip takes $\Delta t=5\ell /2$ while the clock records $\Delta s=2\ell $}{figure.2}{}}
