\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Rotating coordinates with an inertial origin. Left: cross sections of the local coordinate light cone, expressed as velocities in the rotation plane. The dash-dotted circle belongs to the origin ($\beta =0$); the dotted circle to a grid point moving with $\beta =3/5$ along the second local axis. The tangential coordinate light velocities there are $+2/5$ and $-8/5$; their different values reflect the rotating axes. Orthonormal measurements still give light speed $1$. Right: a clock fixed to that grid point runs at $4/5$ of the origin's rate. The open endpoint $\beta =1$ marks a null grid worldline, beyond the region occupied by physical rotating-grid clocks.}}{43}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
